      "headline": "The Volume Knob of Self-Love",
      "keyInsight": "We often treat narcissism like an on-off light switch. We assume someone is either an absolute saint or a cartoon monster. But human personality works much more like a kitchen dimmer switch. A little bit of self-love gives you confidence to speak up at work. It helps you ask for a raise or take pride in a clean home. When that volume knob gets cranked all the way up, healthy self-respect warps into a loud, screeching noise that drowns out everyone else in the room. The counterintuitive truth is that extreme narcissism is not an excess of genuine self-worth. It is a desperate, frantic noise designed to cover up dead silence underneath.",
      "content": "Think about seasoning a pot of soup. A pinch of salt brings out rich flavors and makes the meal delicious. Dump half the shaker in, though, and the entire dinner is ruined. Narcissism exists on the exact same sliding scale. On the low end, you find healthy self-esteem. This is the quiet inner glow that lets you enjoy your own company and brush off minor blunders.\n\nMove slightly higher up the scale, and you meet everyday boastfulness. We all know someone who talks a little too long about their new car or their weekend marathon. It might be mildly annoying, but it does not wreck relationships. They can still listen when you share bad news, and they can still apologize when they step on your toes.\n\nAt the far edge of the spectrum, the system breaks down completely. Here, self-absorption stops being a bad habit and becomes an impenetrable bunker. People at this extreme cannot tolerate the slightest hint that they are ordinary. They do not just want to enjoy the party. They must convince the whole room that they invented the music.",
