      "headline": "The Paper Shield and the Invisible Scratch",
      "keyInsight": "Why does a casual comment cause an explosive meltdown? If an old sedan gets a tiny scratch in a grocery parking lot, the owner shrugs and drives home. But imagine driving a car made entirely of paper-thin Venetian glass. Even a stray pebble or a gentle gust of wind feels like a lethal crash. Narcissists do not explode because they feel superior. They explode because their sense of worth is fundamentally hollow inside. A mild critique feels like a wrecking ball smashing through their living room wall. Their fiery rage is simply an emergency rescue squad rushing to patch over deep, terrifying shame.",
      "content": "Imagine wearing a set of cardboard armor painted to look like solid titanium. From ten feet away, you look invincible. You stride through the neighborhood with your chin up, expecting everyone to admire your glittering suit. But deep down, you know rain will turn your impressive armor into soggy mush in seconds.\n\nThis is what living with fragile self-esteem feels like every single day. Genuine confidence functions like an oak tree with deep roots. When a storm blows through, the branches sway, but the trunk stays firmly planted. Hollow confidence is like an umbrella propped up on gravel. A sudden breeze flips it inside out, sending the owner scrambling in panic.\n\nWhen a fragile person encounters a tiny snub, the mind sounds a five-alarm fire bell. A polite correction from a coworker is not heard as advice. It is processed as complete personal ruin. To stop that unbearable shame from leaking out, they lash out, shift blame, or rewrite history on the spot. The anger you witness is really just smoke escaping from a hidden basement fire.",
